  {
    "idx": 61,
    "doi": "10.1109/cvci66304.2025.11348153",
    "title": "Study on AR-HUD Design in Unprotected Intersection Scenario Under Autonomous Driving",
    "year": 2025,
    "authors": [
      "Chang Wei",
      "Yangbin Jin",
      "Yingying Fu",
      "Yutian Zhang",
      "Yating Su",
      "Wenbo Li"
    ],
    "venue": "2025 9th CAA International Conference on Vehicular Control and Intelligence (CVCI)",
    "is_oa": false,
    "pdf_url": "",
    "landing": "https://doi.org/10.1109/cvci66304.2025.11348153",
    "abstract": "Autonomous driving technology is evolving from assisted driving to fully autonomous driving. However, there are still some technical challenges in the industry that lead to a lack of trust from drivers, which affects driving safety and user experience. As one of the important components of the intelligent cockpit, the head-up display (HUD) technology can improve driving safety and trust in autonomous driving. The combination of HUD and augmented reality (AR) technology, known as AR-HUD, is an important future technological direction. However, the design of AR-HUD faces the problem of high information density, which can easily cause interference and burden to drivers. Therefore, this paper researches ARHUD design schemes based on human-machine trust and visual load. The unprotected intersection scenario is selected as the starting point. Through user-need research, high-demand ARHUD driving-assistance information is screened. The “distance warning for surrounding vehicles” information, which has the highest demand, is used as the essential information. Based on different combinations of “trajectory prediction of ego vehicle” “trajectory prediction of surrounding vehicles” and “blind spot video”, the design and comparative testing of schemes are carried out. Subjective and eye-tracking data are collected. The trust degree and load degree of participants in autonomous driving under different schemes are comprehensively evaluated. Finally, through data analysis, it is found that there is no significant difference in trust among different schemes. The combination of “trajectory prediction of surrounding vehicles” and “blind spot video” has the lowest workload. It can be considered that the combination of these two types of information is relatively optimal, which will bring significant improvement to driving trust and safety in autonomous driving.",
    "local_pdf": "",
    "local_text": "",
    "has_fulltext": false,
    "cited_by": 0,
    "source_list": "list1",
    "ref_key": "wei2025inter"
  },
  {
    "idx": 62,
    "doi": "10.1016/j.aap.2018.01.040",
    "title": "How to warn drivers in various safety-critical situations – Different strategies, different reactions",
    "year": 2018,
    "authors": [
      "Susann Winkler",
      "Juela Kazazi",
