  {
    "idx": 73,
    "doi": "10.2139/ssrn.6351998",
    "title": "A-TTC: A Multimodal Fusion Framework for Personalized Truck Forward Collision Warning via Dynamic Threshold Calibration",
    "year": 2026,
    "authors": [
      "Yibing Wang",
      "Qi He",
      "Jingqiu Guo",
      "Mark Stevenson",
      "Yan Xu"
    ],
    "venue": "",
    "is_oa": false,
    "pdf_url": "",
    "landing": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6351998",
    "abstract": "Heavy trucks experience persistent forward collision risks due to driver heterogeneity and limitations of fixed-threshold Forward Collision Warning (FCW) systems, which fail to adapt to dynamic driving states and multimodal contextual cues. This study assembled a large naturalistic dataset of 9,435 logged FCW triggers from 816 trucks; 3,519 events were video-verified and used for model development. A driver-adaptive FCW framework (A-TTC) is proposed that: i) detects heterogeneous driver states (e.g., distracted, harsh) using a multimodal attention network, ii) predicts driver reaction time and braking distance with a hybrid gradient-boosting plus convolutional-transformer model, and iii) integrates a dynamic time-to-avoidance threshold into FCW decision logic. On naturalistic events, A-TTC increased overall accuracy to 82.67%, and reduced the false-positive rate from 25.16% to 12.21%. In simulations spanning stationary, constant-speed, gradual- and emergency-deceleration scenarios, A-TTC matched or exceeded the best baseline in collision-avoidance success. Overall, adapting FCW thresholds to multimodal, state-aware driver representations substantially reduces nuisance alarms in heavy trucks without sacrificing hazard detection. This research provides a data-driven and driver-adaptive approach to enhancing the safety and personalization of commercial vehicle collision avoidance systems.",
    "local_pdf": "",
